/*!

\page css-styling-applications.html
\title Styling Applications with CSS

\section1 Getting Started Writing CSS

Once you have \l{working-with-styles.html}{created a new style} you will have a
directory containing, among others, a \c{style.css} file. This file is what
Union will load. However, per the recommendations you should not add your style
rules directly to that file. Instead, you should create separate files for
different (groups of) elements and add your style rules there.

For example, to add style rules for the Button element, you can create a file
called \c{button.css}, in your style's \c{content/css/} directory, then add
\c{@import "button.css"} to \c{style.css} and it will include the rules in
\c{button.css} when loading the style.

Then in your \c{button.css} file, you can add CSS rules like this:

\code
button
{
    width: 100px;
    height: 32px;

    background-color: white;
    color: black;
    border: 1px solid black;
}
\endcode

This will result in all your buttons having a default size of 100px wide and
32px tall, with a white background, black text and a black border around it. For
details on which exact properties you can use within style rules, see the
\l{css-properties.html}{Property reference}.

\section1 What You Can and Cannot Style

Union strives to provide a generic "middle layer" that styles can provide styling
information for and that outputs can consume to apply the styling. This middle
layer includes the definition of what elements there are, what properties these
elements have that you can use for styling and what kind of sub-elements there
are. See the \l{elements.html}{Element Reference} for details.

While Union's goal is to give as much freedom to you as style creator to style
things the way you want, there are inherent limitations in what Union can do due
to how the underlying technology works.

The most significant limitation is that the application, and not Union, is the one
that decides the layout of things. This means that, unless otherwise noted, you
will not be able to control the position of elements. For sizes, the application
is also in final control, but you \i{can} provide an initial size for most elements.

The exception to this is that there are certain elements that consist of several
sub-elements. Union is generally in control of the placement of these
sub-elements, which allows you as style creator to influence them.

In addition to these limitations, please also read the
\l{css-support.html}{Supported CSS} to see what kind of properties are and are
not supported.

*/
